We hypothesized that the fault lay in the distance between molecules on which such methods rely. On a given dataset, most features of a general-purpose representation either do not vary or do not relate to the property, yet each of these features adds a term to the distance. Together, these irrelevant features dominate the distance. Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} measures this effect on base representations and on the distances computed from them. We propose instead to select a fixed number of task-relevant features from several representations and to compute distances in the resulting compact space, which we call a hybrid representation. A first version of this approach showed that the selected feature sets also work as ordinary model inputs, without any distance involved, so the question became how to choose the features, which is the subject of Chapter~\ref{chap:method}. Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity} then returns to distances, noise detection and LNL, including noise mitigation, using the hybrid representation as the feature space.

