\section{Introduction}
% [notes moved to thesis_notes_moved.md]


While there have been many investigations into the effect of noise on molecular property prediction, direction comparisons between how different molecular representations responds to label noise remains limited. The choice of molecular representation certainly affects predictive performance. \citet{Deng2023} evaluated 62,820 models and found that ECFPs frequently outperform learned graph embeddings across models. Whether these conclusions translate to noise robustness is our question. Between different types of label noise, \citet{Heid2023} found no difference in model performance between error distributions with the same mean and standard deviation. We expand on this by considering alternative patterns of noise, namely noise concentrated in structural groups or on a sparse subset of samples.

