The ANOVA showed that model architecture and the model--representation interaction term dominate the variance in NDS. SVM and full BNNs stood out as noise-robust regardless of representation, and both rely on strong inductive biases, margin maximization in the case of SVM and weight priors in the case of BNNs. RF and NN-$\beta$ showed the opposite pattern, since they can be robust to noise but only when paired with particular representations, and these representations differ between the two models.

% TODO: review, needs manual review
Although representation is not a dominant determinant of NDS variance, it has an effect through the model--representation interaction term. On SMILES, model choice explains over 91\% of the variance in robustness, compared with 72\% on PDV. Across all six noise strategies, PDV configurations produced the shallowest mean NDS of all representations, with the best values coming from models with strong inductive biases such as SVM and full BNNs. This suggests that PDVs are preferable when dealing with noisy data, and it appears that the compact feature space of PDV complements margin-based and Bayesian regularization. Embeddings performed poorly, particularly when paired with NNs. These patterns are consistent with the importance of the interaction term and indicate that the choice of representation modulates noise robustness within a given architecture.

Taking a closer look at model choice for noise robustness, we ranked all model--representation configurations on QM9 by their Gaussian NDS. Tree-based ensembles dominate the top 10: NGBoost appears five times, XGBoost and RF twice each, and LightGBM once. No neural-network configuration appears the top 10. Rankings are consistent across strategies and the top QM9 configurations maintain their advantage on absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion (ADME) datasets, though SVM configurations rank higher on these ADME datasets than NGBoost.

