Which of the two counts matters depends on the distance. A bit that is off in every molecule does not change the Tanimoto similarity, because bits that are off in both molecules are ignored. A bit that is on in every molecule, however, adds one to both the shared and the total bit counts and so raises every Tanimoto similarity slightly. On hERG $K_i$ all 105 constant ECFP4 bits are off, whereas 1 of the 194 constant ECFP4 bits is on for Caco-2 and 1 of 39 for LogD. On hERG $K_i$, 2 of the 24 constant MACCS features are on. After standardization, a constant feature adds zero to a Euclidean or Manhattan distance. Near-constant features, in contrast, distort these distances, because after standardization they take large values on the few molecules that carry the minority value. A bit that is on in 5\% of molecules becomes 4.36 on those molecules, and a bit that is on in one of the 1,412 hERG $K_i$ molecules becomes 37.56 on that molecule.

