For each dataset and representation, we grouped the molecules whose stored feature vectors are exactly identical and assigned every molecule in such a group to one of three causes. A molecule counts as a repeat if another molecule in its group has the same InChIKey, as a featurization failure if its feature vector is all zeros, and as a distinct molecule otherwise. We call a distinct molecule stereo-only when every molecule sharing its vector has the same InChIKey connectivity block, so that the molecules differ only in stereochemistry. This count requires no model to be fitted.

Every molecule in a tied group must receive the same prediction, and the prediction with the lowest squared error is the group's mean label. The lowest error any model can reach is therefore
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{RMSE}_{\min} = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{G}\sum_{i \in G}\left(y_i - \bar{y}_G\right)^2},
\end{equation}
where $n$ is the number of molecules, $G$ runs over the tied groups and $\bar{y}_G$ is the mean label of group $G$. A molecule that belongs to no tied group adds nothing to the sum, and $\mathrm{RMSE}_{\min}$ is expressed in the dataset's label units. The corresponding best $R^2$ is $1 - \mathrm{RMSE}_{\min}^2 / \mathrm{Var}(y)$. We also report the label spread inside each tied group, defined as its highest label minus its lowest. For scale, repeat p$K_i$ measurements of one compound disagree by about $0.54$ log units \citep{Kramer2012}, so a tied group whose p$K_i$ values differ by more than that is not explained by measurement noise alone. We counted ties on hERG $K_i$ (1,412 molecules after deduplication by InChIKey), LogD (5,028 molecules) and Caco-2 (2,158 molecules), using each dataset's base representations and its augmentation features.

