The structure--activity landscape index of a pair of molecules $i$ and $j$ is
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{SALI}_{ij} = \frac{|y_i - y_j|}{1 - \mathrm{sim}(i,j)}
\end{equation}
\citep{Guha2008}. A tied pair has similarity 1, so its index is infinite whenever the two labels differ, and such a tie is therefore the most extreme activity cliff possible. Some cliffs are thus artifacts of the representation. A method that uses nearest neighbors sees a tied neighbor at distance 0 with a different label. % CHECK: sec:flaws:neighbors is not yet a label in thesis.tex; it comes from another CODE INPUT fragment.
