Two different questions can be asked of a distance. The first concerns all pairs and asks whether distance tracks the difference in property across the whole dataset. Plotting descriptor distance against activity difference for every pair gives the neighborhood-behavior plot of \citet{Patterson1996}, which we summarize by a single number, the Spearman correlation between pair distance and absolute label difference. The second question concerns nearest neighbors only and asks whether each molecule's label can be predicted as the mean label of its five nearest neighbors. The neighbors can carry signal even when the all-pairs correlation is near zero.

% [TRIM 2026-09-30] shortened
We computed both quantities for every base representation of each of the seven regression datasets, that is, the six datasets of Table~\ref{tab:dataset_reps} and HLM clearance. The six comprise the small-molecule datasets and QM9. Representations with only binary features were compared by Tanimoto distance, $1-T$, and all others by Manhattan distance on standardized features, leaving out features that are constant across the dataset. Manhattan distance is defined in the next subsection. For the all-pairs correlation, each dataset with more than 2,000 molecules was reduced to a fixed random sample of 2,000 molecules (seed 0), giving 1,999,000 pairs per dataset. On hERG $K_i$, all 996,166 pairs of the 1,412 molecules were used. For the neighbor prediction all molecules were used, and each molecule's five neighbors were drawn from other scaffold groups, so that no molecule is predicted from its own scaffold.

Across the 70 combinations of dataset and representation, the all-pairs correlation ranged from $-0.04$ (MACCS keys on MDR1--MDCK efflux) to 0.23 (PDV on the same dataset) and was below 0.10 in 44 of the 70. The Spearman correlation of the five-neighbor prediction ranged from 0.29 (Avalon on RLM clearance) to 0.89 (ChemBERTa-2 on QM9), and it was the larger of the two numbers in all 70 combinations. On hERG $K_i$, ECFP4 gives an all-pairs correlation of 0.06, while its five-neighbor prediction reaches 0.72, with an RMSE of 0.63 p$K_i$ against a label standard deviation of 0.92 p$K_i$. Table~\ref{tab:flaws:distance_summary} gives both numbers for three representations per dataset.

Figure~\ref{fig:flaws:herg_pairs} shows why the two numbers differ on hERG $K_i$. Of the 996,166 pairs, 93\% have an ECFP4 Tanimoto similarity below 0.20 and 1.1\% reach 0.55. The median label difference is 0.81 p$K_i$ across all pairs but falls to between 0.20 and 0.30 p$K_i$ above a Tanimoto similarity of about 0.4. Even among the 774 pairs at 0.8 or above, however, 8.3\% still differ by more than 1 p$K_i$. Under Mordred, the closest 5\% of pairs have a median difference of 0.44 p$K_i$, whereas every other 5\% of pairs has a median between 0.78 and 1.11 p$K_i$. The signal therefore sits in a thin tail of close pairs. The all-pairs correlation is set mostly by the other 95\% of pairs, whereas a five-nearest-neighbor prediction looks only at the closest few pairs of each molecule.

A third view asks how often two representations agree on each molecule's five nearest neighbors. We compared ECFP4, one descriptor set and SMI-TED, a learned embedding that is a base representation of every one of these datasets. The descriptor set is Mordred, except on Caco-2, where Mordred is not a base representation and PDV is used instead. HLM clearance does not include ECFP4 among its base representations, so its stored ECFP4 features were used for this comparison only. The neighbor sets are those used for prediction. On hERG $K_i$ any two of the three representations share on average 0.47 to 0.52 of each molecule's five neighbors. On the three Biogen datasets and QM9 they share 0.13 to 0.21, fewer than one neighbor in five (Table~\ref{tab:flaws:neighbor_overlap}).

A fourth view asks whether fingerprint distance agrees with a structural distance. We sampled 500 random pairs per dataset (seed 0) and computed their maximum common substructure (MCS) with RDKit \citep{rdkit}, matching ring atoms only to ring atoms, keeping only complete rings and allowing each pair 2 seconds. The MCS distance is $1 - n/(a+b-n)$, where $n$ is the number of atoms in the common substructure and $a$ and $b$ are the numbers of heavy atoms of the two molecules. Pairs that reached the time limit were left out, namely 5 on hERG $K_i$, 8 on Caco-2 and 6 on LogD. The Spearman correlation between ECFP4 Tanimoto distance and MCS distance ranged from 0.20 (hERG $K_i$ and HLM clearance) to 0.51 (Caco-2 and LogD).

A similarity can also be learned. \citet{Coupry2022} learn a molecular similarity from graph embeddings, but independently of any endpoint, so that it is still not chosen for the property being predicted. Earlier work did choose the similarity for the task. \citet{Zheng2000} predict each molecule's activity as the average over its $k$ most similar molecules, using Euclidean distance over descriptors, and select the descriptor subset by simulated annealing to maximize the leave-one-out $q^2$. \citet{Itskowitz2005} extend this approach to optimize the descriptor subset, the number of neighbors and the neighbor weighting function jointly. Both methods select features for a nearest-neighbor model of one endpoint, whereas the method of this thesis selects features across several representations. Every method in Section~\ref{sec:flaws:consequences} takes a similarity as input and inherits the dilution described here. Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity} (``Similarity in a hybrid'') tests whether a feature set chosen for the task gives a better similarity.

