The exploratory study tested 12 LNL methods on the HOMO--LUMO gap from QM9, using 10,000 molecules and scaffold splits. Gaussian noise with standard deviation $\sigma$ from 0 to 1.0, in steps of 0.1, was added to the training labels only, and the test labels stayed clean. The methods were paired with five representations, ECFP4 (2,048 bits), Sort \& Slice \citep{Dablander2024}, PDV, canonical SMILES and randomized SMILES. Depending on the method, a configuration could include up to four optional components, namely the model's own uncertainty, a distance between molecules, an alternative GP kernel and an alternative loss function. The distances were Tanimoto on ECFP4, Euclidean on PDV and Mahalanobis, and the GP kernels were Tanimoto, RBF and Mat\'{e}rn. In total we tested 221 configurations over method, loss function, distance and representation. Four of the 12 methods, small-loss selection, confident learning, distance-based selection and multistage cleaning, remove suspected molecules and then retrain. These four are noise mitigation methods, and the other eight are noise-robust training methods.

We measured noise robustness by retention, defined as $R^2$ under noise divided by $R^2$ with clean labels, as a percentage. Methods that used a distance did worse than those that did not, and all 20 configurations with the lowest retention used a distance. None of the configurations with a distance reached both a clean $R^2$ above 0.6 and a retention above 80\%. The failure lies in the distance step, since the same methods without the distance component did not fail in this way. Not every use of similarity failed, however, because the heteroscedastic GP compares molecules through its kernel and still held up. The failure is specific to judging one label by the labels of its neighbors.

% [TRIM 2026-09-30] shortened
The methods that held up estimate noise from the model's own loss or uncertainty. DivideMix \citep{li2020dividemix} retained the most, 86.7\% (standard deviation 0.4\%), followed by small-loss selection \citep{Han2018} with 84.2\% (standard deviation 2.1\%) and the uncertainty curriculum \citep{Bengio2009} with 82.7\% (standard deviation 3.5\%). The heteroscedastic GP \citep{liu2020} retained 79.0\%, averaged over 15 configurations (3 kernels and 5 representations). Its $R^2$ was 0.71 with clean labels and 0.56 at $\sigma = 0.6$, the noise level at which its retention was measured. The original study does not state the noise level at which the other methods' retention was measured. Regularization alone retained less, with 73.0\% for Mixup and 74.0\% for sharpness-aware minimization. Under these methods, the choice of representation explained even less variance than in Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness}. Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity} compares twelve LNL methods on the current datasets.

The study used one dataset, one property and one noise type, namely Gaussian noise on every training label, and Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity} repeats the question on more datasets and noise types. The distance step was also added to methods designed without one, so a poor adaptation is an alternative explanation. The recommendation of noise-robust training over noise mitigation (Chapter~\ref{chap:discussion}, ``Limitations'') originates in this study.

% [TRIM 2026-09-30] shortened
There are two possible reasons for the poor performance of the distances. First, a single molecular representation may lack sufficient information for distance calculations, with and possibly even without noise. PDVs encode global molecular properties, ECFP4 contains substructure information and MHG-GNNs encode the molecular graph \citep{kishimoto2023}, and any one of these sources alone may not be sufficient for robust similarity estimation. Second, some of these representations are sparse and contain many features that are not relevant to the molecules of a given dataset (Section~\ref{sec:flaws:similarity}). The evidence here does not separate the two reasons. We sought to address the second with a representation of informative features and low dimensionality, the budget-constrained hybrid representation, which concatenates the top-ranked features by importance from a selection of base representations.
