This chapter asked three questions about the representations used to compare molecules and answered each with a direct measurement.

The first question was how much of a standard representation varies on a single dataset, and the answer is often very little. On hERG $K_i$, 1,815 of the 2,048 ECFP4 features take one value in at least 95\% of the 1,412 molecules (Section~\ref{sec:flaws:constant}). Several representations also give different molecules the same feature vector. ECFP4 gives 56 of the 1,412 hERG $K_i$ molecules a vector shared with another molecule, all of them stereoisomers (``Molecules with identical feature vectors''). No model can separate those molecules, whatever their labels.

The second question was whether distance follows the property. Because a distance sums over every feature, the remaining features dominate the sum when only a few features matter for the property (Section~\ref{sec:flaws:similarity}). Across 70 combinations of dataset and representation, the Spearman correlation between the distance of a pair and its label difference was below 0.10 in 44. % CHECK: NOTES say keep the all-pairs vs nearest-neighbor contrast only if the full runs support it. The numbers below come from distance.tex, computed on the saved features, not from the full runs.
We also predicted each molecule's label as the mean of its five nearest neighbors' labels, and the Spearman correlation of this prediction with the label was higher in all 70 combinations. The useful information therefore sits in a few close pairs rather than in the distance as a whole.

The third question was what happens to methods that rely on distance. In the exploratory LNL study, the configurations that used a distance between molecules did worst under label noise (Section~\ref{sec:lnl_motivation}). Judging a label by its neighbors' labels usually made the model worse and rarely helped at both noise levels (Section~\ref{sec:flaws:neighbors}). It did not always fail, since one neighbor-based configuration gave the best recovery at the higher noise level. The noise was Gaussian and added to every training label, so each molecule's neighbors were noisy too, and Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity} returns to other noise types.

% [TRIM 2026-09-30] shortened
Section~\ref{sec:lnl_motivation} gives two possible reasons for the failure of the distance. Our evidence does not separate the two, and the requirements below address the second, irrelevant features.

A representation that avoids these flaws would need five properties. Its features would be chosen for the task, using only the training labels of each fold. It would be compact, with features that do not vary or do not matter removed before any distance is taken or any model is fitted, and the number of features kept would be searched rather than fixed. It would draw on several representations, since each misses information that the others capture, and plain concatenation meets this requirement but not the previous one (Chapter~\ref{chap:method}, ``Combining representations and the alternatives''). It would work both as a model input and as a space in which to compute similarity. Finally, it would be traceable, so that each chosen feature leads back to its representation and can be read chemically wherever the base feature can.

Feature selection cannot fix every flaw. Molecules that are identical in every base representation stay identical in the hybrid, and a selected hashed bit still merges every substructure that folds into it. Selection also needs enough labeled molecules to estimate which features matter.

These flaws are where the thesis began. Our hypothesis was that similarity as usually computed, with ECFP4 and the Tanimoto coefficient or their variants, carries many features irrelevant to the task. We further expected that methods built on it, such as noise detection and noise mitigation, would suffer as a result. Here a task means a supervised model fitted on a training set. The first version of our method selected the task-relevant features of several representations, with the number of features set by the user, and was built only to compute similarity. The selected feature sets then turned out to work as ordinary model inputs without any similarity step, and the selection itself became the method, the adaptive hybrid representation.

Building this method raises several questions, which Chapter~\ref{chap:method} addresses. They concern how a feature is scored (the importance method), how the budget is split across representations (the allocation method) and how a candidate hybrid is scored (the internal evaluator). They also concern how many features are kept (the budget) and which downstream model the selected features feed. Features are chosen per representation rather than from one pooled ranking, because some datasets prefer features from only the top few representations, and Chapter~\ref{chap:method} develops this argument.

