So far, the most common strategy for combining feature sets has been concatenation, which requires only a single downstream model \citep{Stahlschmidt2022}. Concatenation works best when the feature sets are homogeneous and degrades when they are not \citep{Stahlschmidt2022}. It also obscures which representation is responsible for the score. Deng et al.\ found that appending RDKit2D descriptors to GROVER lowered RMSE on 9 of 24 datasets, yet they still called the practice ``misleading'' when the goal is to measure the contribution of individual representation types \citep{Deng2023}. Late fusion trains a separate model per source and aggregates the predictions \citep{Steyaert2023}, which avoids the dimensionality problem but loses interactions between features from different sources. Recovering those interactions requires intermediate fusion (MOGONET \citep{Wang2021}, cross-attention \citep{Beaude2025}), at the cost of end-to-end training on all sources, so that adding a new source means retraining from scratch. Multiple kernel learning \citep{Gonen2011} operates entirely at the kernel level, so no interpretable feature subsets emerge from it. An example in drug bioactivity prediction is pairwiseMKL \citep{Cichonska2018}. As Steyaert et al.\ noted, there remains no decisive evidence that any of these strategies is broadly superior \citep{Steyaert2023}.

