The hybrid ranks features by the importance scores of a fitted model, as embedded methods do, and then keeps a fixed number of top-ranked features, as a filter does. Classical methods treat all features as one flat list and ignore the representation each feature came from. Their nearest relative to the hybrid is selection over predefined groups, as in the group lasso, which keeps or drops whole groups of variables together \citep{Yuan2006}. The hybrid, in contrast, does not keep or drop whole representations, but splits the budget among them (``Feature allocation methods''). Because only original features are kept, every feature in the hybrid traces back to one representation, which is what makes blueprints possible.

