Here, we present the adaptive hybrid representation, which takes a collection of feature matrices for a given dataset and outputs a feature set customized for a specific prediction task. The inputs can be any type of frozen feature vector, such as fingerprints, descriptor sets, foundation model embeddings, gene expression signatures or histopathology features. Within a given domain, each of these vectors typically carries its own type of information. It is well established in machine learning that one approach rarely suits every problem, and the noise robustness study of Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness} provides evidence of this for NGBoost on clean labels. On QM9, NGBoost's held-out $R^2$ was highest with PDV input features (0.865, median of 10 replicates), with MHG-GNN only 0.013 lower (0.852) and ECFP4 lowest (0.706). On LogD, PDV was again highest (0.673), followed by MHG-GNN (0.658). On hERG $K_i$, MHG-GNN was highest (0.511) and PDV second (0.456), the latter being the median of the 4 of 5 folds whose clean $R^2$ was at least 0.3. On Caco-2, NGBoost appeared to prefer Avalon (0.402), with ChemBERTa at 0.404. Both numbers are medians of only 2 of 5 folds, because the other 3 fell below a clean $R^2$ of 0.3 and were excluded. Over all 5 folds, PDV is highest (0.370), ahead of Avalon (0.287) and ChemBERTa (0.256). NGBoost thus tends to produce better fits from physicochemical properties, yet a different prediction task can override this preference. Accuracy and robustness also favor different representations. On QM9 under Gaussian noise, NGBoost's highest noise robustness (\aucnorm, the normalized area under the curve of $R^2$ against noise level) was on Avalon, at 0.982 against 0.975 for PDV.

But as important as the selection of useful features is, it is also important to filter out the useless features. We could throw the kitchen sink at the models and pool together features across a wide array of representations. Not only would that pose computational challenges, but it also introduces a huge set of irrelevant features that function as noise in downstream applications (Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws}). With all of that in mind, I decided to develop a method that selects the best features for a given data set and model. In theory, it should effectively filter out noisy features and isolate the feature which carry the most relevant information.

