The budget is split between the representations before any feature is picked. Representations differ in width by orders of magnitude, from tens to thousands of features, so in a single ranking over all their features a wide representation can crowd out a narrow one. Importance scores from different types of feature are also not on a common scale. For these reasons, each representation's features are ranked only against each other. The search also includes one pooled allocation method, the global pool, which ranks the features offered by every representation together, so that per-representation and pooled selection are compared instead of assumed.

% CODE INPUT
A hybrid can also be used to measure similarity in two ways. Distances can be computed in an ordinary hybrid, or the search can pick features by how well each molecule's activity is predicted from its five nearest neighbors. Both approaches are defined in the section ``Similarity in a hybrid'' of Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity}.


