The search is given $K$ frozen representation matrices $\mathbf{X}^{(k)} \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times d_k}$, $k = 1, \dots, K$, for the same $n$ samples, whose widths $d_k$ range from tens to thousands of features. It is also given a label vector $\mathbf{y}$ and one group label per sample for cross-validation, for example its scaffold. It further needs the dataset's metric, the task type, one or more downstream models and a list of budgets. From these inputs, it finds which features from which representations best predict for that metric and downstream model. The output is a hybrid feature matrix of at most $B$ features, where the feature budget $B$ is a maximum rather than a target. Uncapped augmentation adds its extra features outside the budget, whereas capped augmentation stays within it. The output also includes a blueprint that records the settings needed to rebuild the hybrid. The search first scores each representation independently and then scores feature importance on training data within each cross-validation fold. It then allocates the budget among representations. Four of the five allocation methods do this according to each representation's standalone performance, while the global pool instead ranks the offered features from all representations together. Candidate hybrid configurations are scored by cross-validation, and we retain the best configuration for each combination of downstream model and budget.

% [TRIM 2026-09-30] paragraph removed, text in cuts file

% TODO: REVIEW MODIFICATION
Inputs from different representations differ in scale, distribution and missingness patterns. Pooling them naively before splitting would leak validation-set statistics into the training procedure, a well-documented source of optimistic bias in feature selection studies \citep{Ambroise2002, Vabalas2019}. All preprocessing therefore runs within each cross-validation fold. When a single representation is scored, we impute missing values with per-feature medians computed on the training fold and drop features that are entirely missing. When features are selected for the hybrid, a missing value is instead set to 0 before standardization. Every feature of the hybrid, binary fingerprint bits included, is standardized to zero mean and unit variance using training-fold statistics only. Binary features are left as 0/1 in one case only, when the search scores a single representation to pick the comparator, whereas a single representation scored on the outer test fold is standardized like the hybrid. A representation wider than 5,000 features is first reduced, inside each fold, to the 500 features on which a ranking model splits most often. It keeps 200 features instead when the search is given fewer than 1,000 molecules. The ranking model is a LightGBM model of 50 trees of depth at most 5, fitted on the fold's training molecules. It ranks features by how many times its trees split on them rather than by gain. This reduction applies only when the search scores a single representation on its own. For such a representation, the comparator is therefore picked on its top 500 (or 200) features and then scored on the outer test fold at full width, while the hybrid search always sees the full width.

% CODE INPUT
Each dataset is split into outer folds, 5 by default. The folds are group-aware, so that no group appears on both sides of a split, and classification folds are also stratified by class. A representation learned from the labels is retrained inside each outer training fold, on its training molecules only. The search then runs its own group-aware cross-validation, with 5 folds by default, on the molecules it is given. Inside each training fold of the search, one group-aware internal split holds out about 20\% of the molecules as whole groups. This split is the first fold of a 5-fold grouped split and is therefore deterministic. When a training fold holds fewer than five groups, both splits use as many folds as there are groups. For example, Koenig expression has five groups, so each outer training fold holds four. The search inside that fold uses 4 folds of three groups each, and the internal split holds out one of the three. Importance and each representation's standalone score come from this training fold only, and the outer test molecules score only the finished hybrid.

% CODE INPUT
For each representation $k$, the importance method gives every feature one score $s^{(k)}_j \ge 0$, and these scores rank features within that representation only. Importance is computed on the internal training part of the fold. The standalone score $r_k$ comes from the internal evaluator, fitted on representation $k$ alone and scored on the internal held-out part in the dataset's metric. We rescale $r_k$ to $\tilde r_k$ so that 0 means ``no better than a trivial predictor'', which makes the budget split independent of the dataset's metric. For Spearman, Pearson, the Matthews correlation coefficient (MCC) and $R^2$, $\tilde r_k = \max(0, r_k)$. For AUROC and the concordance index, chance is 0.5, so $\tilde r_k = \max(0, 2(r_k - 0.5))$. For RMSE, $\tilde r_k = \max(0, 1 - \mathrm{RMSE}_k/\mathrm{RMSE}_0)$, where $\mathrm{RMSE}_0$ is the error of predicting the training mean, and the mean absolute error is rescaled in the same way with the training median as the trivial predictor. For accuracy and weighted F1, $\tilde r_k = \max(0, (r_k - r_0)/(1 - r_0))$, where $r_0$ is the score of always predicting the majority training class. The trivial predictor is scored on the same held-out molecules. A representation whose fit fails receives weight 0 and is recorded as a failure.

% CODE INPUT
An optional representation count $N$ keeps only the $N$ representations with the highest standalone scores on this fold. Each remaining representation receives a weight $w_k = g(\tilde r_k)$, where the function $g$ is set by the allocation method. The exact share of representation $k$ is $b^*_k = B\, w_k / \sum_l w_l$ features, and we round the shares by largest remainder. Each representation first receives $\min(\lfloor b^*_k \rfloor, d_k)$ features, and the leftover features are then given out one at a time in order of largest fractional part, skipping representations already at full width. The result satisfies $\sum_k b_k = \min(B, \sum_k d_k)$ and $b_k \le d_k$, and a representation with $w_k > 0$ keeps at least one feature. If every weight is 0, the budget is split equally and the run reports this, and if no representation can be scored at all, the configuration is refused. The global pool does not use this rounding. Instead, each representation offers candidate features, and one joint ranking keeps at most $B$ of them.

% CODE INPUT
Let $S_k$ be the top $b_k$ features of representation $k$ by $s^{(k)}_j$, among those with $s^{(k)}_j > 0$. The same cut at 0 applies to the features offered to the global pool and to its final ranking. If no feature of a representation scores above 0, its full $b_k$ is taken. The cut exists because tree importance gives exactly 0 to every feature on which no tree split, and filling the budget from those features would pick them by their position in the matrix. The hybrid is $\mathbf{H} = [\mathbf{X}^{(1)}_{:,S_1}, \dots, \mathbf{X}^{(K)}_{:,S_K}]$, with $\sum_k |S_k| \le B$, and it can therefore be narrower than its budget. The matrix $\mathbf{H}$ is then preprocessed inside the fold, as described above.

% CODE INPUT
A configuration assigns one value to every searched setting. For each configuration, we fit the downstream model on the hybrid's training molecules in each fold of the search, score it on that fold's held-out molecules and average over the folds. The winner is picked separately for each pair of downstream model and budget and never across models. Because that score was used to choose among many configurations, it is optimistic \citep{Varma2006, Cawley2010}, and the comparison we report is therefore nested. The whole search runs inside each outer training fold, the winning settings are rebuilt on that fold, and the result is scored once on the untouched outer test fold. Both the winner at each budget and each downstream model's best across budgets are scored in this way. The inner search score exceeds the outer test score by \textbf{[RESULT NEEDED: the gap between inner search score and outer test score, per dataset, in each dataset's own metric]}.

