To examine the selected features more closely and to rebuild the hybrid on new datasets, we produce blueprints. A blueprint is the recipe for the winning hybrid, that is, the search settings needed to rebuild it. Every scored configuration has one, and the search returns the winner's blueprint with its result. On other molecules, a rebuild re-scores the representations and re-picks the features, so it need not recreate the same hybrid. A blueprint stores the budget, the full list of input representations searched and how many of them the recipe used. It also stores the feature importance method, allocation method, internal evaluator and objective. Further runtime information includes the number of folds, the seed, the metric used for scoring and its direction, and the task type (regression or classification). The augmentation settings are stored as well, covering how many extra features to take, from which candidates, and the rule for admitting one. Some information is recorded for later analysis only. This comprises each representation's best score across the downstream models, the number of features each representation received in each fold, and the augmentation features kept in each fold. These records show which representations fed the hybrid and whether this holds across folds. The stored score differs from the standalone score that splits the budget, because it is measured on every molecule the search saw, and a rebuild therefore never reads it.

