The blueprint does not store the selected base-representation features, although it does store how many features each representation received in each fold and which augmentation features were kept. A blueprint can be reused in two ways. The first re-runs the recipe on new labeled data and recomputes importance and standalone scores, so that the features adapt to the new dataset. This costs a new selection and can give a different feature set. The second takes the exact features of a labeled fit from the new molecules. This requires no labels and is therefore the only option for unlabeled molecules, but it assumes that the same features matter on the new data. Re-running the recipe without labels is refused, because every importance score would be 0 and the selection would fall back to feature order. Importance and allocation can also be computed separately within k-means clusters of the molecules' ECFP fingerprints, to ask whether the budget split changes across chemical space.

It also provides a registry of selected features, stating where that feature came from. On some representations, we added an extension where features are mapped back to their function. For instance, ECFP4 and MACCS bits are mapped to SMILES arbitrary target specification (SMARTS) substructures. This blueprint will tell you which of those substructures were selected. This is not possible for learned embeddings for which the blueprint will return the bare representation name and dimension index. This mapping provides a layer of explainability. It can be used both to discover what features are useful for your task and to confirm that the features being selected are in fact lining up with the underlying known chemistry.

