When we began to implement this idea, we expected a fairly straightforward way to select the most informative features, but several questions soon arose. How should the importance of a feature be measured? Given a handful of input representations, how many features should be taken from each? How should a candidate feature set be scored? As with the choice of representation, different prediction tasks preferred different answers to each of these questions. The search therefore treats each question as a setting and chooses its value per dataset.

% CODE INPUT
Every combination of setting values in the grid is built and scored, and nothing is fixed in advance except the values each setting may take. The claim of this chapter is that no single setting suits every dataset, and a grid measures this claim instead of assuming it. The motivation is the familiar result that no learning method is best on every problem \citep{Wolpert1996}, although that result does not prove the claim for hybrids. We search the full product of values, instead of one setting at a time, because the settings interact. For example, the best budget depends on the downstream model, as the nanobody thermostability example below shows.

% CODE INPUT
Six settings are searched, namely the downstream model, the budget, the representation count, the importance method, the allocation method and the internal evaluator. Their values at the full tier and at the fast tier are listed in Table~\ref{tab:search_tiers}. The defaults and the number of configurations for each downstream model are given in Additional files, Section~\ref{app:searchspace}. The importance method is LightGBM gain, TreeSHAP or DeepLIFT. It is searched because importance depends on the model as well as on the data \citep{Dong2020}. The three methods come from different model families and rank features differently, and a different ranking builds a different hybrid. The allocation method decides how the budget is split among the participating representations, and five allocation methods are searched. The internal evaluator scores each representation on its own. It is the matched evaluator (a model of the downstream model's own family), random forest, LightGBM or a deep neural network (DNN). Two further choices are not searched within a single search. The objective is prediction by default, which Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity} replaces with predicting each molecule's label from its nearest neighbors in the hybrid. Augmentation is absent, uncapped or capped. Each objective and each augmentation condition is therefore a separate search.

% [TRIM 2026-09-30] shortened
% CODE INPUT
The downstream model is the model trained on the finished hybrid. Five downstream models are searched, namely random forest, XGBoost, LightGBM, SVM and DNN, and a Gaussian process is added on datasets under 1,000 molecules (``Models and metrics''). The downstream model is searched because it determines what counts as a useful feature and how many features it can use. The best hybrid for one model therefore need not be the best for another.

% CODE INPUT
The budget is the maximum number of features the hybrid may have, and eight budgets are searched (10, 25, 50, 100, 250, 500, 1,000 and 2,000 features). The budget is searched because too few features leave informative ones out, whereas too many add uninformative features that, on small datasets, add room to overfit. Budgets above the number of training molecules are kept. The best budget depends on the downstream model. In a preliminary run on nanobody thermostability with 5 folds, SVM performed best at 300 features, with a Spearman correlation of 0.6384. DNN, LightGBM and random forest performed best at 50 features, and XGBoost at 100. That run used an earlier list of budgets, which included 300 features. \textbf{[RESULT NEEDED: the best budget for each downstream model on nanobody thermostability, from the full runs]}

