Not all features from a given base representation are equally informative, and a variety of techniques can assess each feature's relative importance. The search ranks the features of each representation before choosing any, using an importance method that gives every feature of one representation a single non-negative score. Each representation receives its own freshly fitted model on its own features only, and the downstream model is not used. Only the order within a representation is used, and because these scores are never compared across representations or across methods, no normalization is applied. Scores are computed inside each fold on the internal training part only, which consists of 80\% of that fold's training molecules, split by group. Before fitting, missing values are set to 0 and each feature is standardized to mean 0 and variance 1 on the same molecules. Split-based tree importance is biased toward features with many possible split points \citep{Strobl2007}. This bias matters here, because binary fingerprint bits sit beside continuous embeddings. Neural attributions such as DeepLIFT instead measure how much each feature moves a neural network's output away from its output on a reference input \citep{Ancona2018}.

