We score features using LightGBM gain importance, SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) values for tree ensembles (TreeSHAP) and DeepLIFT (Deep Learning Important FeaTures) \citep{Shrikumar2017}, which come from three families, namely split gain, Shapley values and reference-based attribution. LightGBM gain measures how much a feature reduced the training loss where trees split on it \citep{ke2017lightgbm}. TreeSHAP measures how much a feature moves each molecule's prediction, as an exact Shapley value for tree models \citep{Lundberg2020}. DeepLIFT measures how much a feature moves the output of a small network away from its output on a reference input \citep{Shrikumar2017, Kokhlikyan2020}. The network has two hidden layers of 64 and 32 ReLU units and is trained for 100 full-batch epochs of Adam at a learning rate of 0.001 \citep{Kingma2015}. The reference input is all zeros, which after standardization places every feature at its training mean. For a fingerprint bit, this mean is the fraction of training molecules that have the bit set. For LightGBM gain and TreeSHAP, a representation wider than 2,000 features is pre-screened. Only its 2,000 highest-variance features, with variance measured before standardization, are fitted and the rest score 0, whereas DeepLIFT scores every feature. On Caco-2, the median share of features scoring above 0 was 30\% for LightGBM gain, 70\% for TreeSHAP and 90\% for DeepLIFT. This preliminary median is taken over 10 representations and 3 folds. Table~\ref{tab:importance_methods} summarizes the three importance methods.

