Fixed shares rely on the assumption that many features in a well-performing representation are valuable. This is not always the case, since a strong representation may owe its score to a few features, so that a large share fills with weak ones. Fixed shares also cannot adapt the allocation to feature complementarity, and two representations carrying the same information both receive a share. Of the five methods, only the global pool ranks features from different representations against each other.

