The four fixed-share methods differ only in the weight $w_k$ computed from the rescaled score $\tilde r_k$ (Table~\ref{tab:alloc_methods}), and their settings are fixed for every dataset rather than searched. Linear allocation sets $w_k = \tilde r_k$ and squared allocation sets $w_k = \tilde r_k^{\,2}$, so under both, a representation receives 0 features when its rescaled score is 0. Softmax allocation sets $w_k = \exp\big((\tilde r_k - \max_l \tilde r_l)/T\big)$, with temperature $T = 1$. Rank-decay allocation sets $w_k = e^{-0.3\rho_k}$, where $\rho_k$ is the representation's rank by score, 0 for the best. Under these two methods, no representation receives 0 features because of its score. Since every rescaled score lies between 0 and 1, softmax allocation cannot give one representation most of the budget, and with 10 representations the best takes at most 23.2\% of it. Under rank decay only the order counts, so with 10 representations the best receives 27.3\% and the tenth 1.8\%, whatever the gaps between their scores. Table~\ref{tab:alloc_example} works through one example with a budget of 100 features and three representations with rescaled scores 0.6, 0.5 and 0.2.

