The global pool sizes each representation's offer with squared weights $\tilde r_k^{\,2}$, as shares of a pool of $2B$ candidate features. Each offer is capped at the representation's width in features, and only features scoring above 0 are offered. The offered features are joined into one matrix, on which the same importance method is fitted once, on the internal training part. The hybrid keeps the top $B$ features of this joint ranking, again only those scoring above 0. Each representation's final count is therefore an outcome of the joint ranking rather than set in advance, and there is no minimum of one feature per representation. A representation receives 0 features when its score is 0 or when none of its candidates reach the joint top $B$. Under the global pool, a feature's rank depends on which features the other representations offered, whereas under fixed shares it does not. The cost is one extra importance fit per configuration, on the pooled candidates.

