Features that improve predictions under a random forest may not necessarily help an SVM, and vice versa, because variable importance depends fundamentally on the model, as Dong and Rudin showed \citep{Dong2020}. To address this, we use an internal evaluator, a model fitted inside the search on one representation at a time and separate from the downstream model scored at the end. We search four evaluators, namely random forest, DNN, LightGBM and the matched evaluator, a special case that matches the internal evaluator to the downstream model. No evaluator is chosen in advance to suit the downstream model. Instead, all four are searched and the best configuration wins. Features are standardized on the internal training part of the training fold, where we fit each evaluator before scoring it on the internal validation part in the dataset's own metric.

