Some features are too low-dimensional or too specialized to take part in the budget allocation stage, for instance MACCS fingerprints (167 features) or log-likelihood ratios (3 features). Others, such as mutation features that record differences from a parent antibody, only carry meaning alongside a full representation. Excluding such features from the allocation would lose potentially useful signal, whereas including them would distort the budget distribution because they cannot contribute many features. We therefore handle them as augmentation. We call the finished hybrid $\mathbf{H}$ the base, and we call the feature sets from which the extra features are drawn the donors.

