Donors are set per dataset rather than per domain and are listed in the Augmentation column of Table~\ref{tab:dataset_reps}. Augmentation features range from smaller fingerprints such as MACCS to sets of structural information such as 3D pharmacophores and Coulomb matrix eigenvalues. They also include features that describe only the differences between variants, such as mutation features for antibodies. The Coulomb matrix donor is not a fingerprint but the 50 largest eigenvalues of a matrix built from atomic numbers and 3D distances between atoms. MACCS is usually a donor, serving as one on four of the six small-molecule datasets, and it is a base representation on MDR1--MDCK efflux. QM9 is the exception, since MACCS is not offered there and the topological torsion fingerprint (2,048 features) is a donor, the only wide one. Each donor is small, at most 167 features apart from the torsions, or describes only one aspect such as shape, charge, the mutated positions or one tumor property. Each is therefore a plausible supplement but a weak representation on its own. Evidence from the literature suggests that 3D donors are worth offering. \citet{Bahia2023} modeled six protein--ligand series (461 structures) with 2D, 3D and combined 2D and 3D descriptors, using $k$-nearest neighbors, random forest and lasso. Combining 2D and 3D descriptors gave many more significant models, which the authors attribute to the two descriptor types coding for complementary properties. Their 3D descriptors came from bioactive conformations in the Protein Data Bank, whereas our 3D donors use one generated conformer per molecule.
