The hybrid is compared against the best single base representation used at its full number of features, which asks whether the hybrid beats what a user would otherwise run. This comparator is chosen from the search's own single-representation scores on the training samples of the outer fold, then refit on those samples and scored on the same held-out samples as the hybrid. Because it is chosen separately for each downstream model and each fold, it can be a different representation in each fold. Choosing it on the held-out samples would give it an advantage that the hybrid does not get. The hybrid is held to its budget of base features, to which any augmentation features are added. The comparator has no budget and is therefore often many times wider than the hybrid. Both widths are recorded for every fold, since a difference between the two scores is partly a difference in width as well as in method. The main quantity per fold is the hybrid's score minus that of the best single representation, signed so that a positive value always means the hybrid scored better (for RMSE the sign is flipped).

