Each dataset is scored in the metric its field normally uses, so that its numbers can be compared with published work on the same dataset. Small-molecule, quantum-chemistry and reaction-yield regression use RMSE, which is in the units of the label. Antibody regression uses Spearman $\rho$, because these assays are used to rank candidates and their scales differ between laboratories. Binary classification uses AUROC, which does not depend on a decision threshold. The two nucleotide datasets are the exception and use MCC, which is high only when all four entries of the confusion matrix are good \citep{Chicco2020}. The four PAM50 subtypes, whose class sizes range from 67 to 421 tumors, are scored with support-weighted F1. Table~\ref{tab:datasets} gives the metric of each dataset, and the definitions are given in Chapter~\ref{chap:background}, ``Evaluation methodology''. Metrics are never averaged across datasets or domains.

