The ensemble fits one model per representation and pools their predictions, whereas the hybrid fits one model on one fused feature vector. Joining features before a single model is early fusion, and pooling the predictions of one model per feature set is late fusion \citep{Stahlschmidt2022}. The hybrid runs its own search on the training molecules of each outer fold. Every choice for the ensemble is made on an inner 3-fold group split of the same training molecules, so the held-out fold is used once. The ensemble either fits the same model on every representation or lets each representation use its own best model. Its members are either the top 3 representations or every representation with at least 50 features. The predictions are pooled in one of seven ways. Three are the mean, the median and a 20\% trimmed mean, and two are means after removing outliers found by interquartile range or by median absolute deviation. The last two are a mean weighted by the inner score of each member and the mean of the top 3 members. Classification ensembles pool predicted probabilities, and where the metric reads class labels, a majority vote is also offered. Three controls are included in the comparison. The first is the best single representation, and the second joins all representations and reduces them by PCA to the width of the hybrid. The third is the best pair of model and representation read off the held-out fold, which serves as an upper bound rather than as a fair comparator. Beside each score we report the cost as the number of representations computed at prediction time, the number of models deployed, and the fit and prediction time in seconds. The hybrid saves cost only if it draws on fewer representations than the ensemble. Because an ensemble is the obvious simpler alternative, a result in which it matches the hybrid at lower cost belongs in Chapter~\ref{chap:discussion}, ``Simple is often better''. In QSAR, \citet{Turkina2025} combined six non-hashed fingerprints by