This experiment asks whether importance also helps during training, in addition to its use in selection. It uses the hybrid at 100 features with random forest, LightGBM and XGBoost, the three models that accept per-molecule weights. Importance is computed on the training molecules with the importance method the search picked for that hybrid. The experiment compares the following five conditions:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item No weighting, in which the hybrid is used as selected.
    \item Feature weighting, in which features are multiplied by the square root of their importance after standardizing, with the weights normalized to average 1.
    \item Molecule weighting, in which molecules are weighted by how well their label agrees with their 10 nearest neighbors in the rescaled space.
    \item Combined weighting, which applies feature weighting and molecule weighting together.
    \item Above-median selection, which keeps only the features strictly above the median importance.
\end{enumerate}
The weight of molecule $i$ is $1 - |y_i - \bar y_{N(i)}| / (\max y - \min y)$, clipped to lie between 0.1 and 1. Here $\bar y_{N(i)}$ is the mean label of its 10 nearest neighbors by Manhattan distance. This comparison is not fully held out, because the configuration comes from a search over all molecules and only the feature selection is redone per fold. Molecule weighting is a noise-mitigation method that down-weights suspect labels. Tree ensembles are unchanged when a feature is multiplied by a positive constant, because their splits depend only on the order of values \citep{Hastie2009}. Feature weighting therefore differs from no weighting in one respect only. Features with zero importance receive weight zero, become constant and drop out, while every other feature is only rescaled, which the three tree models ignore. The rescaling does matter for molecule weighting, because it changes which molecules are nearest neighbors. Neighbor-based filtering of labels is reviewed in Section~\ref{sec:flaws:neighbors}, and here the molecule is down-weighted rather than removed. We found no QSAR study that down-weights training molecules whose labels disagree with their nearest neighbors. Learned per-sample training weights for noisy labels come from general machine learning \citep{shu2019metaweightnet}. \textbf{[RESULT NEEDED: one result line]}.

