\section{Discussion}
% [notes moved to thesis_notes_moved.md]
% - TODO(lit): neither source above is a study of when combining gives no gain. A source that reports it directly is still needed.

% [TRIM 2026-09-30] shortened
% TODO: review, needs manual review
Ambroise and McLachlan \citep{Ambroise2002} showed the extreme case of overfitting through feature selection. An SVM achieved near-zero cross-validated error on random labels when genes were selected on the full dataset before cross-validation. When selection was nested inside each fold, the median error was 11 out of 40, close to the 50\% expected by chance. Vabalas et al.\ \citep{Vabalas2019} showed that the bias introduced by pre-selection can exceed the bias from hyperparameter tuning. Cawley and Talbot \citep{Cawley2010} argued that feature selection is itself a form of model selection whose complexity cost must be accounted for. The hybrid search follows the standard remedy of computing all importance scores within each cross-validation fold, on training data only. The problem of search degrees of freedom \citep{Tibshirani2015} nevertheless remains, since searching over many possible feature subsets adds complexity even when each individual selection is unbiased. The budget acts as a hard capacity constraint. Selecting 100 features from a pool of 5,000 limits the space of possible selections far more tightly than $L_1$ regularization over the same pool. The reason is that the feasible set grows polynomially in the budget while the continuous parameter space of a regularized model is unbounded \citep{Ng2004}. The features being selected are, moreover, frozen embeddings rather than parameters being fitted. We consider choosing which pre-existing knowledge to trust a less expressive operation than learning new features. In support of this view, Kumar et al.\ \citep{Kumar2022} showed that frozen embeddings fed through a linear probe can outperform full fine-tuning by up to 10\% in out-of-distribution accuracy, because fine-tuning distorts pretrained structure. Finally, stability can be measured directly with the Nogueira index (``Comparators, controls and ablations''). If selection were overfit, the selected feature sets would differ widely from fold to fold.

\section{Limitations}
% [notes moved to thesis_notes_moved.md]
% - TODO(lit): source for SchNet outperforming frozen embeddings on 3D quantum-mechanical properties. The sentence has no citation. The SchNet paper (Sch\"utt et al., NeurIPS 2017) and Faber et al. 2017 do not compare SchNet with frozen or pretrained embeddings.

% TODO: review, needs manual review
The adaptive hybrid representation has several limitations. For small datasets, below about 200 samples, the hybrid search cannot reliably outperform the best single source. We suspect that this is because the internal validation set within each fold shrinks to roughly 20 samples, at which size the evaluator cannot distinguish genuine improvements from noise, and tuning cannot fix this. At the other extreme, frozen embeddings have a ceiling, since no signal can be added beyond what the inputs already contain. This ceiling binds on tasks where the true signal requires end-to-end learning from raw inputs, for example certain 3D quantum-mechanical properties, where SchNet outperforms any frozen embedding by a wide margin. The method naturally accommodates embeddings from sequence-trained models such as ESM-2 and ProtBERT. Encoding raw sequences directly as one-hot or $k$-mer features, however, disperses the positional and compositional information that makes sequences useful in the first place. Integrating 20 to 50 foundation models into a single pipeline, each with its own dependencies, GPU requirements and version constraints, is also difficult engineering. Embeddings need to be computed only once per dataset and are cached, but the initial integration burden is real and scales with the number of models in the pool. Finally, on endpoints with high experimental noise, even a well-constructed hybrid may not improve on simpler baselines. Kramer et al.\ \citep{Kramer2012} documented that the experimental uncertainty in heterogeneous public $K_i$ data is approximately 0.54 log units, which sets a natural accuracy ceiling of $R^2 \approx 0.81$ regardless of model sophistication. The budget constraint plausibly guards against overshoot, but it cannot push past this noise floor.


