We use hybrids for similarity in two ways. The first is an ordinary hybrid, in which the search of Chapter~\ref{chap:method} selects features that help a downstream model and distances are then computed on those features. The second is a similarity hybrid, which is produced by the same search but ranks each candidate by how accurately each molecule's activity is predicted from its five nearest neighbors in the hybrid. No downstream model is fitted during the search for a similarity hybrid. The two searches share their importance methods, allocation methods, budgets and folds, and differ only in the score that ranks candidates. The neighbor score also sets each representation's share of the budget, so no internal evaluator is used. For the budget split, this score is computed on a validation set drawn from each fold's training molecules, whereas the score that ranks whole candidates uses the fold's held-out molecules. Every representation in the dataset's set is a candidate, and the number of representations (2, 3, 5 or all) is not searched. Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} showed that the representation determines which molecules count as neighbors. The similarity hybrid tests whether selecting features for the quality of the neighbors addresses this problem more directly than selecting them for prediction. \textbf{[FIGURE NEEDED: D6.1, two pipelines sharing a first half (base representations, importance, allocation, hybrid). Top: hybrid, downstream model, dataset metric. Bottom: hybrid, 5 nearest neighbors from other scaffold groups, distance-weighted mean activity, Spearman or rescaled AUROC. Caption: same search, different score.]}

