As in Chapter~\ref{chap:method}, each feature is z-scored with the mean and standard deviation of the training molecules only. The distance is the Manhattan distance on the z-scored features, as defined in Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws}. Because every z-scored feature contributes one term of similar size to this distance, the number of features that a representation contributes to a hybrid acts as its weight. The influence of a representation on the distance is therefore roughly its number of features divided by the budget, so the allocation method also weights the representations within the distance. The neighbor score used by the search applies no importance weighting to the z-scored features. \textbf{[RESULT NEEDED: confirm Manhattan distance and one-over-distance neighbor weighting once the design experiment crossing distance, neighbor weighting and number of neighbors has run in full]}

% CODE INPUT
To rank whole candidates, the score is computed on the held-out molecules of each outer fold, with other held-out molecules rather than training molecules serving as neighbors. The scaffold groups of the held-out molecules are split into up to 5 parts, or fewer when there are fewer groups. The 5 nearest neighbors of each molecule are drawn from the other parts and hence from other scaffold groups, because a neighbor with the same scaffold is an easy match that would inflate the score. Each neighbor is weighted by the inverse of its distance:
\begin{equation}
\hat{y}_i = \frac{\sum_{j\in N_5(i)} y_j / d_{ij}}{\sum_{j\in N_5(i)} 1/d_{ij}}.
\end{equation}
A neighbor at distance 0 receives all of the weight. On a regression dataset, the score is the Spearman correlation $\rho(\hat{\mathbf{y}}, \mathbf{y})$. On a binary classification dataset, $\hat{y}_i$ is the distance-weighted fraction of neighbors in class 1, and the score is $2(\mathrm{AUROC} - 0.5)$, where AUROC is the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve. This rescaling places chance at 0 under both scores, which matters because the budget split divides by the score. The modelability index of \citet{Golbraikh2014} (Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws}) is a related published measure, but it uses a single nearest neighbor and a hard class match, whereas our score retains the graded fraction of neighbors. The score thus measures the extent to which the similar property principle (Chapter~\ref{chap:background}) holds in the hybrid.

% CODE INPUT
A fold is not scored when it has fewer than 10 held-out molecules, fewer than 2 scaffold groups, or constant labels or predictions, and such a fold is omitted from the mean rather than counted as 0. Because AUROC is defined for two classes, multiclass labels are not scored either, which excludes the PAM50 breast cancer subtypes (4 classes) and the trastuzumab HER2 multiclass dataset (3 classes). The folds of the trastuzumab HER2 binary dataset are cut along edit-distance shells around the wild-type CDR-H3. A held-out block can therefore contain a single shell, in which case no molecule has a neighbor from another group. The similarity hybrid is not searched on these three datasets, but it is searched on every other dataset in the thesis, including the antibody and other non-molecule datasets.


\paragraph{Feature importance weighting.}

Beyond simply using the feature importance scores for allocation, we investigate whether feature weighting by importance during distance computation improves noise detection. For a feature importance vector $w$, weighted distances are computed by scaling the standardized features: $x' = x \odot w$ under Manhattan distance ($x \odot \sqrt{w}$ under Euclidean), with negative importances set to 0 and $w$ rescaled to average 1. 


