\subsubsection{Recovery Rate}
% [notes moved to thesis_notes_moved.md]

For noise detection accuracy, we compute precision, recall, and F1 score, treating noise injection as ground truth.

% [TRIM 2026-09-30] shortened
% TODO: REVIEW MODIFICATION -- formula checked against the code on 2026-09-29: the experiments in this chapter compute recovery per downstream model, in the dataset's own metric, as a percentage, and leave it undefined when the clean-to-noisy gap is 2% of the clean score or less (tests/experiments/noise/n11_estimation_remediation_grid.py:300, 1457, 1511; same in n07, n32). The R^2 / random forest on ECFP4 version describes the exploratory study only (Table tab:code_input_neighbor_recovery). Your original paragraph is kept commented out below.
Each downstream model $m$ is trained and tested three times, once on the clean training labels, once on the noisy labels and once on the labels after noise estimation and correction. These runs give the scores $s^{m}_{\text{clean}}$, $s^{m}_{\text{noisy}}$ and $s^{m}_{\text{denoised}}$, respectively, in the dataset's own metric and averaged over folds. The recovery rate is the share of the lost performance that correction recovers,
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{Recovery}_m = 100 \times \frac{s^{m}_{\text{denoised}} - s^{m}_{\text{noisy}}}{s^{m}_{\text{clean}} - s^{m}_{\text{noisy}}}\ \%,
\label{eq:recovery}
\end{equation}
which reads the same way whether a higher or a lower value of the metric is better. Each model is normalized by its own gap between the clean and the noisy score. When that gap is no more than $2\%$ of the clean score, the noise did too little damage to be recovered, and the recovery rate is left undefined. A recovery rate of $100\%$ indicates complete restoration of clean performance, and negative values indicate that the corrected labels gave a worse score than the noisy labels.
