The limits of the hybrid search itself, including its run time, repeating the search and reproducibility, are set out in the Limitations section of Chapter~\ref{chap:method}. This thesis set out to mitigate label noise, and our conclusion is that correcting label noise without ground truth is a hard problem with no clear general solution. Every experimental label carries an unknown amount of noise, so there is no clean copy against which to check a correction, and finding a noisy label is easier than correcting it (``Comparison across noise types'', Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity}). What remains possible is outlier detection and the detection of systematic errors with a known structure, such as a shift shared by all labels from one source or clipping at a detection limit. In the assay experiments, ``clean'' means that no noise was added rather than that the labels are true, and only QM9 is close to noise-free. The injected noise conditions are also only models of noise. Real data mix them in unknown amounts, so a method that works under one condition may fail on real data. \textbf{[RESULT NEEDED: per noise condition, how many LNL methods, and how many noise-mitigation methods, improved on the uncorrected model, and where recovery went negative.]}

