\paragraph{\texorpdfstring{hERG $K_i$.}{hERG Ki.}} Records for the hERG potassium channel (ChEMBL target CHEMBL240) were retrieved from ChEMBL \citep{Zdrazil2023} through its web interface. The query keeps records with standard type $K_i$, a pChEMBL value present, an exact relation (``$=$''), and no curator data-validity comment. Records reported as ``$<$'' or ``$>$'' a value are therefore never included. Only binding assays (ChEMBL assay type B) are kept. The label is the pChEMBL value, $\mathrm{p}K_i = -\log_{10} K_i$ with $K_i$ in molar units. Repeat measurements of one compound are merged into their median, and compounds whose repeats have a standard deviation above 1.0 p$K_i$ unit are removed, following \citet{landrum2024}. The structures are then standardized and deduplicated by InChIKey. Because repeats are merged on ChEMBL's own SMILES before standardization, two ChEMBL entries that standardize to the same molecule are not merged by median; the InChIKey step keeps the first of them. In a query run in September 2026, 1,815 $K_i$ records reduce to 1,812 with a pChEMBL value and 1,737 from binding assays. These give 1,415 compounds, 226 of them measured more than once and none removed by the standard-deviation rule. The InChIKey step removes 3 more, leaving 1,412 compounds with p$K_i$ from 4.07 to 9.41. The file read by the experiments was pulled on 1 February 2026; the ChEMBL release was not recorded at the time. Re-running the same query in September 2026 against ChEMBL release 37 gives a byte-identical file of 1,415 compounds.

