The adaptive hybrid representation is implemented in the KIRBy repository. It is written in Python and works on feature matrices computed beforehand, so the hybrid search can be applied to representations from any domain. The repository contains the hybrid search itself, which comprises feature importance scoring, the allocation of a feature budget across representations, internal evaluation and a store of every configuration evaluated for each dataset. It also contains loaders for the datasets in Appendix~\ref{app:datasets} and generators for the molecular, antibody, nucleotide, reaction and gene-expression representations. The neighbor-based similarity analyses, the learning-with-noisy-labels methods of Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity} and every experiment script are included as well. Label noise is added through NoiseInject, so the noise definitions are the same as in Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness}. A GPU is not needed for the hybrid search, but it speeds up the pretrained encoders.

